course: dictionary
lesson: 14657
title: Mostly or mainly: predominantly
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: «Predominantly» is a formal word that means mostly or mainly. It tells you that most of something is one kind, though not necessarily every part of it. The «-ly» ending is a clue: it is an adverb, so it describes how something is.
explain: «The park is predominantly grass, with a few trees by the gate.» Most of the park is grass. The trees are still there, so predominantly does not mean all. Swap in mostly: «The park is mostly grass» still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
explain: In maths problems, predominantly often hints that more than half of a group is one thing. «The class is predominantly girls» means that more than half the children are girls, though a few boys may be there too.
fact: Predominantly means mostly or mainly.
fact: It is a formal word, so you will meet it more in school writing and reports than in everyday chat.
fact: «Predominantly» tells the reader that most, but not all, of something is like this.
fact: «The forest is predominantly pine trees» means pine trees are the main kind of tree there, with some others mixed in.
fact: In maths, predominantly can hint at more than half of a group.
exercise: «The island is predominantly rocky, with only a few sandy beaches.» What does predominantly mean? | *Mostly | Suddenly | Only a little | Never
exercise: «The choir was predominantly children, with two adults at the front.» What does this tell you? | *Most of the choir were children | Every single person in the choir was a child | There were no children in the choir | Only two children sang
exercise: Maya says, «My bag is predominantly red.» What does she mean? | Not red at all | *Mostly red, with some other colours too | Only the very top is red | Red with no other colours at all
test: «The shelves are predominantly full of books, but there is one shelf of toys.» What does predominantly mean here? | Slowly and carefully | Totally full, with no toys at all | Hardly any books at all | *Mainly books, though not every shelf
test: «Out of 20 children, 13 wore blue, so the team was predominantly blue.» What does the word hint at? | Exactly half the team wore blue | Fewer than five wore blue | *More than half the team wore blue | Nobody wore blue
exam: «In a class of 24, 17 children are left-handed, so the class is predominantly left-handed.» What does predominantly suggest about the left-handed children? | Exactly 12 children in the class | Fewer than 5 children in the class | None of the children in the class | *More than half of the class
